---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
const title='3 Things About Listing Your Property Sellers Often Overlook';
const description='Before listing your property, understand three things sellers can overlook: agent compensation, negotiated buyer-side costs and concessions, and the ongoing cost of owning a property while it sits.';
const pubDate=new Date('2026-09-22');
const image="/images/3%20Things%20Sellers%20Don't%20Think%20About.png";
const faq=[
 {q:'Do real estate agents still get paid if a home sells below the original list price?',a:'Agent compensation depends on the listing agreement. If compensation is percentage-based, it is generally calculated from the actual sale price rather than whether the seller achieved the original asking price. Broker compensation is negotiable and not set by law.'},
 {q:'Can a seller pay costs connected with the buyer?',a:'Yes, depending on the negotiated transaction. A seller may agree to concessions for certain buyer costs and may also authorize compensation to a buyer agent. These are negotiable, not mandatory.'},
 {q:'What costs can continue while a property is listed?',a:'Depending on the property, taxes, insurance, utilities, mortgage interest, HOA charges, lawn care, maintenance, security, and repairs can continue while the seller still owns it.'},
 {q:'Is the list price the same as what the seller takes home?',a:'No. The final amount a seller receives depends on the actual sale price and the expenses, credits, compensation, concessions, liens, taxes, and other items that apply to that specific transaction.'}
];
---
<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head>
<BaseHead title={title} description={description} image={image} type="article" pubDate={pubDate} author="CashAsIs" faq={faq}/>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/styles.css"/>
<style>.secret{border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:14px;padding:22px;margin:18px 0;background:#fbfcfd}.secret-num{font-size:13px;font-weight:900;letter-spacing:1.3px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#f26d21;margin-bottom:7px}.secret h2{margin:0 0 9px}.net-box{border-radius:14px;padding:24px;margin:25px 0;background:#0D1B2A;color:white}.net-box h2{color:white;margin-top:0}.net-box p{color:#e5ebf2}</style>
</head><body>
<Nav/>
<section class="legal-hero"><div class="in"><span class="badge">Seller Guide</span><h1>3 Things About Listing Your Property Most Sellers Don't Think About</h1><p class="meta">The sale price gets the attention. What you actually walk away with matters more.</p></div></section>
<main class="legal-wrap"><article class="legal-card article">
<p class="eyebrow"><a href="/blog/">&larr; More Seller Resources</a></p>\n<img class="article-hero" src={image} alt="3 things about listing most sellers do not think about" width="1200" height="630" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" />
<div class="article-body">
<p>If you are comparing a direct cash offer with listing your property, it is easy to focus on one number: <strong>“What could I sell it for?”</strong></p>
<p>But a possible list price is not the same thing as the amount you ultimately receive. A traditional sale can involve negotiated compensation, concessions, transaction expenses, and months of continued ownership costs.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><b>BEFORE YOU COMPARE OFFERS, COMPARE THE WHOLE TRANSACTION.</b><br/>What matters is not only the sale price. It is what applies to your transaction and what you ultimately walk away with.</p></div>

<div class="secret">
<div class="secret-num">Thing #1</div>
<h2>Your agent's compensation does not depend on you getting your original asking price</h2>
<p>Your listing agreement controls how your listing broker is compensated. If the agreement uses percentage-based compensation, that amount is generally based on the actual sale price—not on whether the property sold for the original list price you hoped to receive.</p>
</div>

<div class="secret">
<div class="secret-num">Thing #2</div>
<h2>Costs connected with both sides of the deal can affect your net</h2>
<p>Depending on the negotiated transaction, a seller may agree to concessions that help cover certain buyer costs. A seller may also authorize compensation to a buyer's agent. Neither is automatically required, and the amounts are negotiable.</p>
<p>Examples of concessions can include certain closing expenses, loan-related costs, inspection-related items, repairs, taxes, or other negotiated costs. The point is simple: <strong>the contract price is not automatically the same as the amount you take home.</strong></p>
</div>

<div class="secret">
<div class="secret-num">Thing #3</div>
<h2>The property can keep costing you every month you still own it</h2>
<p>Listing a property does not stop the expenses of owning it. Depending on your situation, property taxes, insurance, utilities, mortgage interest, HOA charges, lawn care, maintenance, security, and repairs can continue until the property actually closes.</p>
<p>A longer sale does not guarantee a lower final price, but more time can mean more ownership expenses. Those costs are worth considering when comparing a direct offer with the possible outcome of listing.</p>
</div>

<div class="net-box">
<h2>Your list price isn't your take-home.</h2>
<p><strong>Potential sale price</strong><br/>minus the expenses and negotiated amounts that actually apply to your transaction<br/>minus the costs of continuing to own and maintain the property until closing<br/><strong>= a more useful estimate of what you may actually walk away with.</strong></p>
</div>

<h2>How does that compare with your CashAsIs offer?</h2>
<p>If you already have a CashAsIs offer, compare that actual offer with the estimated <strong>net outcome</strong> of your other option—not simply with a hoped-for asking price.</p>
<p>With a direct CashAsIs purchase, you are selling as-is rather than preparing the property for a retail listing. CashAsIs does not charge you a listing-agent commission to buy your property, and our purchase is not dependent on a traditional buyer getting mortgage approval. Your specific purchase agreement and closing documents control the actual terms.</p>

<div class="callout"><p><b>ASK THE BETTER QUESTION:</b><br/>Not just “What could I list it for?”<br/><br/><strong>“What will I actually walk away with, how long could it take, and what will I spend or deal with before I get there?”</strong></p></div>

<h2>Why should selling your property have to mean paying more just to get it sold?</h2>
<p>For some owners, listing is the right fit. For others, the repairs, preparation, uncertainty, negotiated expenses, and time are exactly what they want to avoid. Understanding the full transaction lets you compare your choices more clearly.</p>
<p>Next, see <a href="/understanding-your-cash-offer/">what your CashAsIs offer means</a>, compare a <a href="/blog/cash-offer-vs-listing-house-houston/">cash offer with a traditional listing</a>, or review our <a href="/seller-faq/">Seller FAQ</a>.</p>
</div>
<section class="faq"><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>{faq.map((f)=><details><summary>{f.q}</summary><p>{f.a}</p></details>)}</section>
</article></main><Footer/>
<script is:inline src="https://link.msgsndr.com/js/external-tracking.js" data-tracking-id="tk_61336d42285a45a29bdc44b9a0947ddf"></script>
</body></html>